{"answer":"Relevant results on medical knowledge graphs for clinical LLMs include both KG construction/grounding work and studies of when KG grounding helps.

On linking patient evidence to external medical knowledge, MM-KG builds a multimodal typed graph with explicit alignment edges between patient observations (from EHR text, imaging, genomic, biospecimen) and biomedical concepts, enabling query-conditioned retrieval for downstream LLMs or GNNs, and tests that removing the knowledge relation reduces ranking performance back to a no-knowledge baseline [2]. For temporal clinical reasoning, ChronoMedKG introduces a temporally grounded biomedical KG with evidence-linked triples tied to onset/progression components, plus the ChronoTQA benchmark showing retrieval can rescue failures on temporal questions [6]. For clinical diagnosis with LLMs, surgical KG-LLM training is studied via multiple training paradigms and optimization-geometry diagnostics, identifying “surgical alignment” regimes that produce sparse updates and improved reasoning quality [1].

Differences: [2] focuses on multimodal evidence-to-knowledge links and traceable contributions; [6] adds a time axis for longitudinal reasoning; [1] focuses on how KG signal is integrated during LLM training; [3] finds KG grounding helps mainly for out-of-training knowledge (it may not improve known/public facts on clinical QA).", "grounded":true}
